  "topic": "setsid Function",
  "explanation": "The setsid function is a system call in C that creates a new session and sets the calling process as the session leader. It is used to isolate a process from its parent session, detach it from any controlling terminal, and prevent it from receiving signals intended for the original session, which is essential for creating daemon processes or managing process hierarchies in Unix-like systems.",
  "syntax": "int setsid();",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <unistd.h>",
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "int main() {",
    "    if (setsid() == -1) {",
    "        perror(\"setsid failed\");",
    "        return 1;",
    "    }",
    "    printf(\"New session created successfully\\n\");",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program calls setsid to create a new session for the process. If the call succeeds, it prints 'New session created successfully', demonstrating how setsid detaches the process from its original session and controlling terminal, as indicated by the function's return value and error handling."
}
