  "topic": "fork() System Call",
  "explanation": "The fork() system call in C is used to create a new process by duplicating the existing one. It allows for concurrent execution of processes, which is essential for multitasking, process management, and building programs that handle multiple tasks simultaneously in Unix-like systems.",
  "syntax": "pid_t fork(void);",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <unistd.h>",
    "#include <sys/types.h>",
    "int main() {",
    "    pid_t pid = fork();",
    "    if (pid == 0) {",
    "        printf(\"Child process\\n\");",
    "    } else if (pid > 0) {",
    "        printf(\"Parent process\\n\");",
    "    } else {",
    "        printf(\"Fork failed\\n\");",
    "    }",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program demonstrates the fork() system call by creating a child process from the parent. When executed, the child process prints 'Child process', while the parent prints 'Parent process'. If fork() fails, it prints 'Fork failed', showing how the call duplicates the process and returns different values to parent and child for identification."
}
